## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests clean 0/1 DP layering with an additional teleport operation, requiring careful handling to avoid double-counting and efficient O(mn) time complexity with minimal extra memory overhead.

## Key Concepts
- 0/1 DP layering with an additional operation constraint
- Row/column prefix maxima for efficient teleport transition computation
- Path reconstruction under dual-state DP representation

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSum(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Initialize first cell
  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = grid[0][0];

  // Fill DP tables
  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;

      // Regular moves without teleport
      dp0[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp0[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp0[i][j-1] : 0
      ) + grid[i][j];

      // After teleport or using teleport now
      dp1[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp1[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp1[i][j-1] : 0,
        // Teleport from any cell in same row/column
        ...getTeleportOptions(dp0, i, j)
      ) + grid[i][j];
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend DP with a cooldown constraint and to backtrack trades.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-state DP with cooldown
- 1-based transaction counting, backtracking
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitWithCooldown(prices, k) {
  const n = prices.length; if (!n || k===0) return 0;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  let no = new Array(k+1).fill(0);
  let hold = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  let cool = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  hold[0] = -prices[0];
  for (let i=1;i<n;i++){
    let nno=no.slice(), nhold=hold.slice(), ncool=cool.slice();
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      nno[t] = Math.max(nno[t], cool[t]);
      nhold[t] = Math.max(nhold[t], no[t] - prices[i]);
      if (t>0) ncool[t] = Math.max(ncool[t], hold[t-1] + prices[i]);
    }
    no=nno; hold=nhold; cool=ncool;
  }
  let best=0; for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) best=Math.max(best,no[t],cool[t]);
  return best;
}
```

