## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model partitioning with a simple per-block objective and reconstruct optimal cuts. It reveals DP formulation, range queries, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP across two dimensions (prefix and blocks)
- Range maximum precomputation or on-the-fly tracking
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCostMax(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  // precompute max for all ranges
  const rangeMax = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let cur = 0;
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      cur = Math.max(cur, A[j]);
      rangeMax[i][j] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.min(i,m);j++){
      for(let t=j-1; t<=i-1; t++){
        const val = dp[t][j-1] + rangeMax[t][i-1];
        if(val < dp[i][j]){
          dp[i][j] = val;
          par[i][j] = t;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, j = m;
  while(j>0){
    const t = par[i][j];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = t;
    j--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][m], partitions: cuts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(n m) time using monotonic queues?  
- How would you adapt if each chapter cost were max(A[t..i-1]) + c*(i-t)?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests mastery of DAG dynamic programming, vertex-disjoint path packing, and careful state design to enforce disjointness while meeting exact cardinality constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological ordering enables DP by ensuring all dependencies are processed first
- Vertex-disjoint paths are guaranteed by tracking endpoint-specific states
- Path reconstruction requires storing predecessor pointers during computation
- Complexity: O(Mk + Nk) time, O(Nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode
const topo = topologicalSort(dag);
const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
const pred = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

for (const v of topo) {
  dp[v][0] = 0;
  const bestPrev = Math.max(...dp[v]);

  for (const u of dag.inEdges(v)) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
        // Extend existing path
        if (dp[u][t] + val[v] > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = dp[u][t] + val[v];
          pred[v][t] = u;
        }
        // Start new path
        if (t < k && bestPrev + val[v] > dp[v][t + 1]) {
          dp[v][t + 1] = bestPrev + val[v];
          pred[v][t + 1] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

const answer = Math.max(...dp.map(v => v[k]));
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constrained path DP with an extra state (turns). Encourages explicit DP state design, directional transitions, and plan for path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with 4D state (i, j, t, dir)
- Transition logic depends on last direction
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Edge cases when k is large vs. maximum turns (n+m-2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>
    Array.from({length:m}, ()=>
      Array.from({length:k+1}, ()=> Array(2).fill(NEG))))
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
        for(let dir=0;dir<2;dir++){
          const cur = dp[i][j][t][dir];
          if(cur===NEG) continue;
          // move right
          if(j+1<m){
            const nt = t + (dir===0?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i][j+1][nt][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][nt][0], cur + grid[i][j+1]);
          }
          // move down
          if(i+1<n){
            const nt = t + (dir===1?0:1);
            if(nt<=k) dp[i+1][j][nt][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][nt][1], cur + grid[i+1][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = NEG;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
    ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n-1][m-1][t][0], dp[n-1][m-1][t][1]);
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce space to O(n*m*k)?
- How does the solution change if k is unconstrained or if a minimum number of turns is required?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on subsequences with transition costs; requires deriving a 2D DP with O(n^2 m) time and backpointers. Edge cases: m=1 yields 0. Consider tie-breaking on reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on subsequences; 2D states; O(n^2 m) time; backtracking via parents; rolling arrays for space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubseq(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){
    for(let t=2; t<=m; t++){
      for(let p=0; p<i; p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[p]);
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, end = -1;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){ if(dp[i][m] < best){ best = dp[i][m]; end = i; } }
  const idx = []; let t=m, cur = end;
  while(t >= 1){ idx.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  idx.reverse();
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP with non-overlapping selections and per-subarray costs, mirroring real scenarios like batch processing, budget allocation, or ad-slot optimization where exactly k intervals are chosen from a sequence.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping k-interval DP
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Maintaining a running best to optimize transitions from O(n) to O(1)
- Reconstruction of exact intervals

## DP Recurrence
Let pref[i] = sum of first i elements (pref[0] = 0).

**Transition:**
dp[i][t] = max(
  dp[i-1][t],                                    // Don't end a subarray at i
  best[t] + pref[i]                              // End a subarray at i
)

where best[t] = max_{1 ≤ l ≤ i} (dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1])

**Initialization:**
dp[0][0] = 0, dp[0][t>0] = -∞

**Answer:** dp[n][k]

## Reconstruction
Track which choice was made at each dp[i][t]. If dp[i][t] came from dp[i-1][t], no subarray ends at i. Otherwise, find l where dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1] = best[t], and record subarray (l..i).

## Complexity
- Time: O(nk) with O(1) transitions using running best
- Space: O(nk) for dp table, O(nk) for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = [0];
  for (let x of A) pref.push(pref[pref.length - 1] + x);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip position i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 0; // 0 = skip
      }

      // Start/extend subarray ending at i
      if (t > 0) {
        let best = -Infinity;
        for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]) - c;
          if (val > best) best = val;
        }
        if (best > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = best;
          choice[i][t] = 1; // 1 = take subarray ending at i
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const subarrays = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (choice[i][t] === 1) {
      let l = 1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[j - 1]) - c;
        if (val === dp[i][t]) {
          l = j;
          break;
        }
      }
      subarrays.unshift([l - 1, i - 1]); // Convert to 0-indexed
      t--;
    }
    i--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Optimized O(nk) Version
The inner loop can be optimized by maintaining a running best:
```javascript
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i - 1][t - 1] - pref[i - 1]);
    dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], best[t] + pref[i] - c);
  }
}
```
